eloping pedagogical skills, understanding diverse learning needs, and fostering an inclusive educational environment. This is ideal for students aspiring to hold future roles as educators, researchers, or clinical supervisors in academia or educational settings within the field of communication disorders. This student employment opportunity supports the development of skills aligned with the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) Career Readiness Competencies. Through this role, students will strengthen the following competencies: Communication: Students build communication skills by practicing clear, respectful, and professional verbal, written, and interpersonal communication in a workplace setting. Critical Thinking: Students build critical thinking skills by applying judgment, solving problems, prioritizing tasks, maintaining accuracy, and using feedback to improve their work. Professionalism: Students build professionalism by practicing dependability, accountability, confidentiality when applicable, workplace etiquette, and respectful service in a workplace setting. Technology: Students build technology skills by using department systems, software, digital tools, equipment, or university platforms to complete tasks accurately and efficiently. Essential Duties & Responsibilities TAs are responsible for assisting faculty with teaching and administrative tasks, including but not limited to: • Assisting faculty with classroom instruction, records, and assignments • Leading discussion sections (if TA is available during class) • Meeting with students during TA Student Support Hours/Review Sessions (plan for 1 hr /week) • Conferencing with students individually or in small groups • Record an audio sample for class use • Delivering one-course lecture or guest lectures (if TA is available during class) • Leading one group project or discussion (if the TA is available during class) • Assist with grading transcriptions (answer key provided) • Track students’ submissions on Canvas and report to faculty • Preparing in-class activity materials • Recording and calculating grades • Providing feedback on assignments • Enforcing laboratory rules and procedures • Proctoring examinations • Taking attendance or monitoring participation • Preparing and distributing course materials (if the TA is available during class) • Ordering course textbooks and monitoring supply • Meeting with faculty 1x/week • Completing administrative tasks (e.g., photocopying, printing) • Identifying evidence-based resources that can support course content • Communicate student concerns/needs with faculty Qualifications MUST BE A STUDENT AT SAINT XAVIER UNIVERSITY Must be Federal Work-Study eligible Must Include a Resume and Cover Letter The Graduate Assistant will support foundational undergraduate coursework in phonetics and language development. Responsibilities include grading assignments, assisting with skill-based learning (e.g., transcription, analysis), supporting in-class activities, and holding office hours. Strong attention to detail and foundational knowledge in speech and language development are required. Additional Expectations We inspire success by working together to provide meaningful, personalized service in a spirit of excellence. SXU seeks candidates who deliver value-added services in a responsive, collaborative, effective, and respectful manner. The University is committed to diversity and encourages applications from individuals with a wide variety of backgrounds and experiences. Saint Xavier University affirms its position as a Catholic institution, inspired by the heritage of the Sisters of Mercy, and asserts its rights to employ persons who subscribe to the mission, vision, and core values of the University. Saint Xavier University is an Equal Opportunity Employer that makes all decisions regarding recruitment, hiring, promotions and all other terms and conditions of employment without discrimination on the grounds of race, color, creed, sex, religion, national or ethnic origin, age, physical or mental disability, veteran status or other factors protected by law. Hiring decisions will be based on the bona fide occupational qualifications of each applicant. Employer Saint Xavier University Address 3700 West 103rd Street Chicago, Illinois, 60655 Phone 773-298-3452 Website http://www.sxu.edu Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Employment Opportunities Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other acc